{"73739":{"y":1962,"f":"approx","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1961–63","h":10,"osm":"way/36977572","listEntry":1255252,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1255252","qid":"Q7063678","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nottingham%20Playhouse%2C%20Wellington%20Circus%2C%20Nottingham%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%204110935.jpg","archive":[{"file":"Nottingham Playhouse, 1967 - geograph.org.uk - 1537550.jpg","year":1967,"author":"John Sutton","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Playhouse","architects":[["Peter Moro","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Moro"]],"cat":"Nottingham Playhouse","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"The Playhouse began in 1948 as a repertory theatre in a former cinema on Goldsmith Street. Its present building, a modernist theatre designed by Peter Moro, opened here in 1963. In 2001 Anish Kapoor's Sky Mirror was put up between the theatre and Wellington Circus, and in 2004 the building was restored with a Heritage Lottery Fund grant.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Playhouse","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Playhouse","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Peter Moro","note:Peter Moro"]],"links":["Peter Moro"],"when":"1948","made":"1963","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Playhouse","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Playhouse","kind":"Wikipedia"},"madeSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Playhouse","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Playhouse","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Nottingham Playhouse"},{"layer":"people","text":"The architect Peter Moro designed the Playhouse.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"Peter Moro","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Moro","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Peter Moro","sources":[{"title":"Nottingham Playhouse","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Playhouse","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Peter Moro","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Moro","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Nottingham Playhouse","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Playhouse","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1911","whenSrc":{"title":"Peter Moro","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Moro","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1}],"sum":"Nottingham Playhouse is a theatre in Nottingham, England. It was first established as a repertory theatre in 1948 when it operated from a former cinema in Goldsmith Street. Directors during this period included Val May and Frank Dunlop.","sumSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Playhouse","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Playhouse"},"open":[{"what":"theatre","web":"https://www.nottinghamplayhouse.co.uk/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15623,52.95394]},"73787":{"y":1906,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","alts":[{"y":1909,"f":"exact","src":"Wikipedia"},{"y":1908,"f":"exact","src":"Wikidata"}],"h":10,"osm":"way/40324301","listEntry":1270608,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270608","qid":"Q4710403","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/AlbertHallNottingham.JPG","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Hall,_Nottingham","architects":[["Albert Edward Lambert","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Edward_Lambert"]],"cat":"Albert Hall, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"The first Albert Hall, a temperance hall by Watson Fothergill, opened in 1876 and from 1902 was a Wesleyan Methodist mission; it burnt down on 22 April 1906. Albert Edward Lambert designed the present hall, dedicated in 1909, and it stayed a Methodist mission until 1982. It is now a conference and concert venue.","source":{"title":"Albert Hall, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Hall,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Watson Fothergill","note:Watson Fothergill"]],"links":["Watson Fothergill"],"when":"1909","whenSrc":{"title":"Albert Hall, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Hall,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Albert Hall"},{"layer":"people","text":"Helen Watts, the daughter of the vicar of Lenton, joined Nottingham's suffragettes after hearing Christabel Pankhurst speak in 1907. In 1909 she served a month in Holloway, was arrested here at the Albert Hall in July and let go without charge, and went on hunger strike in Leicester gaol.","source":{"title":"Helen Watts","url":"https://map.mappingwomenssuffrage.org.uk/items/show/256","kind":"Mapping Women's Suffrage","read":"2026-11-14"},"who":"Helen Watts","own":1},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect","who":"Albert Edward Lambert","source":{"title":"Albert Edward Lambert","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Edward_Lambert","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"The Albert Hall is a conference and concert venue situated close to the centre of the city of Nottingham in England.","sumSrc":{"title":"Albert Hall, Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Hall%2C_Nottingham"},"open":[{"what":"theatre","web":"https://www.alberthallnottingham.co.uk/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15636,52.95432]},"73856":{"y":1437,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":10,"osm":"way/120036353","kind":"pub","listEntry":1271441,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1271441","qid":"Q7716394","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The%20Bell%20Inn%20pub.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bell_Inn,_Nottingham","cat":"Bell Inn, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"Tree-ring dating of its timbers suggests the Bell Inn was built around 1420, with cellars cut into the sandstone. It claims to be the oldest pub in the city.","source":{"title":"The Bell Inn, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bell_Inn,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"c. 1420","whenSrc":{"title":"The Bell Inn, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bell_Inn,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"The Bell Inn"},{"layer":"pubheritage","text":"CAMRA, the Campaign for Real Ale, rates the interior of the Bell Inn as of some regional importance in its inventory of historic pub interiors, which records the old bars, snugs and fittings that survive. The rating gives the interior no legal protection.","source":{"kind":"CAMRA","title":"Bell Inn: historic pub interior","url":"https://camra.org.uk/pubs/bell-inn-nottingham-162702"}}],"sum":"The Bell Inn is a pub in Nottingham, England. Completed from around 1437, it claims, along with Ye Olde Trip to Jerusalem and Ye Olde Salutation Inn, to be the oldest pub in the city. In 1982 the pub became a Grade II listed building.","sumSrc":{"title":"The Bell Inn, Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bell_Inn%2C_Nottingham"},"open":[{"what":"pub","web":"https://www.greeneking.co.uk/pubs/nottinghamshire/bell-inn"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.15207,52.95338]},"73862":{"y":1752,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":10,"osm":"way/120036349","kind":"retail","listEntry":1246247,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1246247","qid":"Q17553820","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bromley-house-nottingham-facade-sept2013.png","architects":[["Sir Robert Taylor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Taylor_%28architect%29"]],"stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"A Georgian town house was built here in 1752. The Nottingham Subscription Library, founded in 1816, moved in during 1821. In 1836 a meridian line was laid so that the library's longcase clock could keep Nottingham time, 4 minutes and 33 seconds behind Greenwich, and it still does. In 1841 Alfred Barber opened the first photographic studio in the Midlands in its attics.","source":{"title":"Bromley House Library","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromley_House_Library","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1752","whenSrc":{"title":"Bromley House Library","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromley_House_Library","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Bromley House"},{"layer":"people","text":"Edith Ransom (1872–1949) was the first woman president of Bromley House Library.","source":{"title":"List of Notable Nottingham Women","url":"https://nottinghamwomenshistory.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/List-of-Notable-Nottingham-Women.pdf","kind":"Nottingham Women's History Group","read":"2026-11-14"},"who":"Edith Ransom","own":1},{"layer":"people","text":"Ellen Harrington was the first woman librarian of Bromley House, from 1943 to 1983. Her portrait hangs here, in a room named after her.","source":{"title":"List of Notable Nottingham Women","url":"https://nottinghamwomenshistory.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/List-of-Notable-Nottingham-Women.pdf","kind":"Nottingham Women's History Group","read":"2026-11-14"},"who":"Ellen Harrington","own":1},{"layer":"people","text":"George Green, the mathematician, was a miller: his family's windmill in Sneinton is restored and named after him.[^s1] He was a member of the subscription library at Bromley House.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"George Green (mathematician)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Green_(mathematician)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"George Green","sources":[{"title":"Bromley House Library","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromley_House_Library","kind":"Wikipedia"},{"title":"Green's Mill, Sneinton","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green%27s_Mill,_Sneinton","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"George Green (mathematician)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Green_(mathematician)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Bromley House Library","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bromley_House_Library","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1793","whenSrc":{"title":"George Green (mathematician)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Green_(mathematician)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect-osm","who":"Sir Robert Taylor","source":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/120036349","kind":"OpenStreetMap"}}],"sum":"A library at Bromley House. The building was built in 1752, according to OpenStreetMap. It is Grade II* listed and was designed by Sir Robert Taylor.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.15231,52.95354]},"73894":{"y":1877,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":10,"osm":"way/291697300","kind":"commercial","listEntry":1270498,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270498","qid":"Q6744638","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Malt%20Cross%20Upstairs.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malt_Cross","architects":[["Edwin Hill","https://leftlion.co.uk/features/2014/11/the-history-of-the-malt-cross-nottingham/"]],"stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"The site was once an inn called the Roebuck. In 1877 a music hall was built here, to designs credited to Edwin Hill, under a high arched glass roof on laminated wooden arches. It took its name from a monument that once stood in the market square, and it is one of the few Victorian music halls to survive.","source":{"title":"Malt Cross","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malt_Cross","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1877","whenSrc":{"title":"Malt Cross","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malt_Cross","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Malt Cross"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect-osm","who":"Edwin Hill","source":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","url":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/291697300","kind":"OpenStreetMap"}}],"sum":"The Malt Cross is a building in Nottingham, England. It is situated on St James Street in Nottingham city centre, off the Old Market Square. The building was built in 1877 and is one of only a few Victorian music halls still standing.","sumSrc":{"title":"Malt Cross","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malt_Cross"},"open":[{"name":"Malt Cross","what":"pub","web":"https://www.maltcross.com/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.15246,52.95313]},"74174":{"aka":["Church of St Peter With St James","St Peter with St James Church"],"y":1100,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","alts":[{"y":1450,"f":"century","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","text":"15th century"},{"y":1250,"f":"century","src":"Wikidata"}],"h":16,"osm":"way/85698784","kind":"church","listEntry":1255013,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1255013","qid":"Q7595269","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/StPetersNottingham.jpg","archive":[{"file":"Peter de Wint - A View of Nottingham from the Trent - 11826.jpg","year":1850,"author":"Peter De Wint","licence":"Public domain"}],"article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Peter%27s_Church,_Nottingham","cat":"St Peter's Church, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"A stone church stood here by about 1180, and parts of it survive in today's building, one of the three mediaeval parish churches of Nottingham.","source":{"title":"St Peter's Church, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Peter%27s_Church,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"c. 1180","whenSrc":{"title":"St Peter's Church, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Peter%27s_Church,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"St Peter's Church"}],"sum":"St Peter's Church, formally The Church of St Peter with St James, is an Anglican parish church in the city centre of Nottingham, England. It is part of the parish of All Saints', St Mary's and St Peter's, Nottingham. The church is Grade I listed by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport as a building of outstanding architectural or historic interest.","sumSrc":{"title":"St Peter's Church, Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Peter%27s_Church%2C_Nottingham"},"open":[{"what":"place of worship","web":"https://www.nottinghamchurches.org/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.14851,52.95229]},"74840":{"y":1926,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","alts":[{"y":1929,"f":"exact","src":"Wikidata"}],"h":21.0,"osm":"way/67187268","kind":"civic","listEntry":1270582,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270582","qid":"Q7063627","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nottingham%20Council%20House.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Council_House","architects":[["Thomas Cecil Howitt","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Cecil_Howitt"]],"cat":"Nottingham Council House","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"The Council House, designed by Thomas Cecil Howitt, opened in 1929 on the site of the old Exchange at the head of the Old Market Square.[^s1][^s1] The sculptor Joseph Else named the two stone lions on its steps Agamemnon and Menelaus.[^s1][^s1] Little John, the hour bell in its dome, weighs over 10 tonnes, and its strike can be heard 7 miles away.[^s1][^s1]","source":{"title":"Nottingham Council House","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Council_House","kind":"Wikipedia"},"sources":[{"title":"Nottingham Council House","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Council_House","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Nottingham Council House","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Council_House","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"weblinks":[["Thomas Cecil Howitt","note:Thomas Cecil Howitt"],["old Exchange","note:The Exchange"]],"links":["Thomas Cecil Howitt","The Exchange"],"when":"1929","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Council House","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Council_House","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Council House","see":[["The Exchange","p664"]]},{"layer":"market","text":"From 1283 the borough of Nottingham sent two members to Parliament. In the mid-18th century the great landowners, the Duke of Newcastle for the Whigs and Lord Middleton for the Tories, held sway, and the town usually returned one of each. In 1885 the old two-member seat was abolished, and the city was divided into three seats of one member each: Nottingham East, South and West.","source":{"title":"Nottingham (UK Parliament constituency)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_(UK_Parliament_constituency)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1885","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham (UK Parliament constituency)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_(UK_Parliament_constituency)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Three seats for Nottingham, 1885"},{"layer":"people","text":"The architect Thomas Cecil Howitt designed the Council House.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"Thomas Cecil Howitt","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Cecil_Howitt","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Thomas Cecil Howitt","sources":[{"title":"Nottingham Council House","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Council_House","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Thomas Cecil Howitt","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Cecil_Howitt","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Nottingham Council House","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Council_House","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1889","whenSrc":{"title":"Thomas Cecil Howitt","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Cecil_Howitt","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1},{"layer":"people","pin":"p39","who":"Henry Kirke White"}],"sum":"The Nottingham Council House is the former city hall of Nottingham, England. The 200 feet high dome that rises above the city is the centrepiece of the skyline and presides over the Old Market Square, which is also referred to as the \"City Centre\". The building is Grade II* listed, and has retail units at street-level.","sumSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Council House","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Council_House"},"open":[{"name":"Nottingham Tourism Centre","oh":"Mo-Sa 09:30-17:30","web":"http://www.visitnottingham.com/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.14843,52.95344]},"74992":{"y":1865,"f":"exact","src":"OpenStreetMap","h":10,"osm":"way/16464923","kind":"theatre","listEntry":1255233,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1255233","qid":"Q7777438","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Theatre%20Royal%2C%20Nottingham%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%20997109.jpg","dyk":[{"text":"The world premiere of Agatha Christie's The Mousetrap took place here, in October 1952.","source":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","architects":[["Charles J. Phipps","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps"]],"cat":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"Built for the lace manufacturers John and William Lambert, who wanted a prestigious theatre for the town, to designs by Charles J. Phipps; its portico of six Corinthian columns closes the view up Market Street. It opened on 25 September 1865 with Sheridan's The School for Scandal, and in 1897 Frank Matcham remodelled its auditorium.","source":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["Charles J. Phipps","note:Charles J. Phipps"]],"links":["Charles J. Phipps"],"when":"1865","whenSrc":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Theatre Royal"},{"layer":"people","text":"The theatre architect Charles J. Phipps designed the Theatre Royal.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"C. J. Phipps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Charles J. Phipps","sources":[{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"C. J. Phipps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal,_Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1835","whenSrc":{"title":"C. J. Phipps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1},{"layer":"people","pin":"p82"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect","who":"Charles J. Phipps","source":{"title":"Charles J. Phipps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Phipps","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"The Theatre Royal in Nottingham, England, is a theatre venue in the heart of Nottingham City Centre and is owned by Nottingham City Council as part of a complex that also includes the city's Royal Concert Hall. The Theatre Royal attracts major touring dramas, opera, ballet, West End musicals and an annual pantomime.","sumSrc":{"title":"Theatre Royal, Nottingham","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal%2C_Nottingham"},"open":[{"name":"Yarn","what":"pub","web":"https://www.castlerockbrewery.co.uk/pubs/yarn/"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Old Market Square","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/nottinghams-old-market-square.html"}],"xy":[-1.15132,52.95526]},"74994":{"y":1982,"f":"exact","src":"Wikidata","h":10,"osm":"way/40324339","qid":"Q7063688","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Royal_Concert_Hall_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7866093.jpg","credit":{"file":"The Royal Concert Hall - geograph.org.uk - 7866093.jpg","year":2024,"author":"John Sutton","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall","architects":[["RHWL","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RHWL"]],"cat":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"In 1898 Frank Matcham designed and built the Empire Palace of Varieties here. It closed for the last time in 1958 and was demolished for road-widening in 1969. On 27 November 1982 the Royal Concert Hall, designed by the Renton Howard Wood Levin Partnership, opened on the site, with Elton John as its first performer.","source":{"title":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1898","made":"1982","whenSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"madeSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Royal Concert Hall"},{"layer":"people","tpl":"architect","who":"RHWL","source":{"title":"RHWL","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RHWL","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall is a concert hall in the English city of Nottingham. It is owned by Nottingham City Council and is part of a complex that also includes the city's Theatre Royal. The Royal Concert Hall's striking modern architecture has proved to be a city landmark at the heart of Nottingham City Centre, opposite the more recently built The Cornerhouse complex.","sumSrc":{"title":"Nottingham Royal Concert Hall","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nottingham_Royal_Concert_Hall"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.1517,52.95579]},"76275":{"h":10,"osm":"way/100083900","qid":"Q7354324","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rock_City_music_venue_and_club%2C_Talbot_Street_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2913448.jpg","credit":{"file":"Rock City music venue and club, Talbot Street - geograph.org.uk - 2913448.jpg","year":2012,"author":"Roger Templeman","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"stories":[{"layer":"market","text":"The building opened in 1876 as the Alexandra Skating Rink. Rock City opened in December 1980, with the Undertones playing the first gig, and holds 2,000 people.","source":{"title":"Rock City (venue)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_City_(venue)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1980","whenSrc":{"title":"Rock City (venue)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_City_(venue)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Rock City"}],"sum":"Rock City: a nightclub on Talbot Street.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15404,52.95613]}}